package components

// The wiki page: one page of a campaign, inserted into the shell's centre slot.
//
// This is a placeholder that establishes the seam, not the final article. P5
// restyles the shell and P6 fills in the surrounding chrome — the campaign
// navigation, the table of contents, the sibling list, the rail that describes
// the campaign rather than the instance. What is here is the part that has to be
// right before any of that exists: one `<h1>` naming the page, the sanitised body
// in the position the stylesheet will style, and the error state under the same
// heading so a reader who cannot load a page is still told which page failed.
//
// The view model is declared here rather than in viewmodels.go because that file
// is not this work item's to edit, and a route that owns its markup but not its
// model would otherwise have no way to be built. It moves to viewmodels.go with
// the rest of them when the next change touches that file; nothing outside this
// package depends on where it is.

// WikiPageView is the centre slot for `/c/{slug}/wiki/{path}`.
type WikiPageView struct {
	// Shell is the chrome. Its Instance field also fills the right rail, so a
	// caller sets it once.
	Shell ShellView
	// Heading is what the page calls itself: its base name. Always set, including
	// on the failure state — a reader who cannot load a page needs to be told which
	// page they were trying to load.
	Heading string
	// Body is the page's sanitised HTML with its references' addresses written
	// in. Empty when Failure is set.
	Body string
	// Failure replaces the body with the error state when the page could not be
	// produced. A pointer, so "a page with no prose" and "a page that could not be
	// loaded" cannot be the same value.
	Failure *LoadFailure
}

// WikiPage is the centre slot for a campaign's page.
templ WikiPage(view WikiPageView) {
	@Shell(view.Shell, WikiArticle(view), InstanceRail(view.Shell.Instance))
}

// WikiArticle is the page itself: the heading, then either the body or the error
// state under it.
//
// The heading belongs to the route rather than to the state, which is what makes
// "exactly one <h1> per page" (UI §7.2) structural here the way it is on the
// campaign list: a reader who lands on the error state still gets told where they
// are, and the state below it does not have to carry an `<h1>` of its own.
//
// The rail is the instance rail rather than an empty slot: UI §4.4 gives a
// campaign route a rail that describes the campaign, and that panel is P6's. An
// empty `<aside>` would be a landmark with nothing in it, which is worse than a
// panel describing the instance, and a shell that could render an empty rail is a
// shell where "is this the campaign shell?" has two right answers.
templ WikiArticle(view WikiPageView) {
	<h1 id="page-heading" data-testid="page-title">{ view.Heading }</h1>
	if view.Failure != nil {
		@LoadError(*view.Failure)
	} else {
		// @templ.Raw is the one unescaped interpolation in this package, and the
		// input is required to be the render pipeline's sanitised output and
		// nothing else. Escaping it instead would show a reader the page's markup,
		// which is a page nobody can read; and letting anything other than
		// sanitised HTML reach it would undo the two layers `content/policy.go`
		// exists to provide. The route is the only caller, and it gets this value
		// from `content.Renderer.Render` and from nowhere else.
		<div class="page-body" data-testid="page-body">
			@templ.Raw(view.Body)
		</div>
	}
}